 Physique du Globe de Paris). Placed on the seabed and ocean floor, these seismometers independently record acoustic and seismic waves. The origin of these signals can be natural, generated by earthquakes, or artificial, produced by sources such as compressed air guns. These devices, accessible to the entire French scientific community, can locate earthquakes or determine the structure of underwater layers. Deployed at sea up to six kilometers deep, they can record seismic waves for almost a year. For more information: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://parc-obs.insu.cnrs.fr/spip.php?article130\">https://parc-obs.insu.cnrs.fr/spip.php?article130</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"23 May 2012 (upload date)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Olivier Dugornay (Ifremer)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"160419313":{"pageid":160419313,"ns":6,"title":"File:GEOFLAMME cruise - Underwater photo of an Ocean-Bottom Seismometers (OBS) on the seafloor (Ifremer 00754-86644).jpg","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":1633492,"width":1920,"height":1080,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/GEOFLAMME_cruise_-_Underwater_photo_of_an_Ocean-Bottom_Seismometers_%28OBS%29_on_the_seafloor_%28Ifremer_00754-86644%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":1013,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/GEOFLAMME_cruise_-_Underwater_photo_of_an_Ocean-Bottom_Seismometers_%28OBS%29_on_the_seafloor_%28Ifremer_00754-86644%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":1013},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/GEOFLAMME_cruise_-_Underwater_photo_of_an_Ocean-Bottom_Seismometers_%28OBS%29_on_the_seafloor_%28Ifremer_00754-86644%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GEOFLAMME_cruise_-_Underwater_photo_of_an_Ocean-Bottom_Seismometers_(OBS)_on_the_seafloor_(Ifremer_00754-86644).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=160419313","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"During the 2021 cruise of the Geochemistry, Fluids, Acoustic Anomalies, and Magmatic Activity Following the Mayotte Eruption (GEOFLAMME) program, the Institut Fran\u00e7ais de Recherche pour l'Exploitation de la Mer's Victor 6000 remotely operated vehicle took this photo of one of the OBSs (Ocean-Bottom Seismometers) deployed with the ballast cage during the MAYOBS 18 cruise. The OBS was found within about 5 meters of where it was expected on the basis of the position provided by the USBL system (ultrashort baseline acoustic positioning system).","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"1 January 2021 (upload date)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Ifremer","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"157456924":{"pageid":157456924,"ns":6,"title":"File:Test des sismom\u00e8tres OBS (Ocean Bottom Seismometer) en bassin d'essais (Ifremer 00556-66761 - 44081).jpg","index":7,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":6207148,"width":2651,"height":3943,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Test_des_sismom%C3%A8tres_OBS_%28Ocean_Bottom_Seismometer%29_en_bassin_d%27essais_%28Ifremer_00556-66761_-_44081%29.jpg/1920px-Test_des_sismom%C3%A8tres_OBS_%28Ocean_Bottom_Seismometer%29_en_bassin_d%27essais_%28Ifremer_00556-66761_-_44081%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2677,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedi